explanation:
Molar mass of MgCl₂:


24+2(35.5)=95 g mol^{-1}


Moles of formula units:


n=(9.5 / 95)=0.1 mol


Each formula unit yields three ions: one Mg²⁺ and two Cl⁻.


N_{ions}=0.1×3×6.022×10^{23}



N_{ions=1.8066×10^{23}}


The tempting answer B counts only two ions per formula unit, overlooking the magnesium ion.

---

Every Al³⁺ ion has:


13-3=10 electrons


Number of ions in 0.2 mol:


0.2×6.022×10^{23}


Total electrons:


0.2×10×6.022×10^{23}
=1.2044×10^{24}


The tempting answer C uses 13 electrons per ion, which applies to a neutral aluminium atom, not Al³⁺.

---

Molar mass of ammonia:


M(NH₃)=14+3(1)=17 g mol^{-1}


One mole contains 6.022×10^{23} molecules. Therefore:


m_{one molecule}
=(17 / 6.022×10^{23)}



m≈2.823×10^{-23 g}


The tempting answer D multiplies by Avogadro’s number rather than dividing by it.

---

Number of sodium ions:


0.25(6.022×10^{23})
=1.5055×10^{23}


Every sodium ion contains 11 protons. Ionisation changes the number of electrons, not the number of protons.


N_{protons}
=11(1.5055×10^{23})



N_{protons=1.65605×10^{24}}


The tempting answer A is the number of sodium ions, not the number of protons.

---

MgCl₂→ Mg^{2++2Cl⁻}


One mole of magnesium chloride produces one mole of magnesium ions and two moles of chloride ions, giving three moles of ions in total. The tempting answer A confuses the initial amount of formula units with the total amount of ions after dissociation.

